# Remote Work Policy
**Daisy Health**
**Policy ID:** HR-RW-001
**Effective Date:** January 1, 2025
**Last Reviewed:** June 1, 2025
**Owner:** People Operations

---

## Purpose

Daisy Health is a virtual-first primary care company. While our care is delivered online, our people still need clear guidelines about where they work, especially when crossing state or country lines. This policy outlines who can work remotely, from where, and under what conditions.

---

## Scope

This policy applies to all full-time and part-time Daisy Health employees, including clinical and non-clinical staff. Contractors and vendors are governed by separate agreements.

---

## 1. General Remote Work Eligibility

All Daisy Health employees are considered remote-first by default. However, remote work eligibility depends on:

- **Role type** — clinical vs. non-clinical (see Section 3)
- **State or country of work** — Daisy Health must be registered to operate in that location
- **Employment status** — full-time, part-time, or temporary
- **Performance standing** — employees on a performance improvement plan (PIP) may have remote work privileges reviewed

Employees must notify People Operations before changing their primary work location, even temporarily.

---

## 2. Approved Work Locations

### Domestic (United States)

Daisy Health is currently registered to employ workers in the following states:

- California, New York, Texas, Florida, Washington, Illinois, Massachusetts, Colorado, Georgia, Arizona, Virginia, Oregon, Pennsylvania, Ohio, North Carolina

Employees wishing to work from a state **not on this list** must submit a Remote Work Location Request at least **30 days in advance**. Approval is not guaranteed and depends on tax, legal, and compliance review.

### International

Employees may not work outside the United States for more than **10 consecutive business days** without prior written approval from their manager and People Operations. Extended international remote work (more than 10 days) requires:

- Legal review for work authorization and tax implications
- IT security review and VPN compliance confirmation
- Written approval from the VP of People

Daisy Health does not currently support permanent international remote work arrangements.

---

## 3. Clinical Staff — Additional Requirements

Clinical employees, including physicians, nurse practitioners (NPs), physician assistants (PAs), registered nurses (RNs), clinical pharmacists, and behavioral health counselors, have additional requirements beyond the general policy.

### Licensure Requirements

Clinical staff must hold an **active, unrestricted license** in the state where the patient is located at the time of the clinical encounter — not necessarily the state where the employee is physically working.

However, if a clinician is physically working from a state different from their primary credentialed state, they must also:

- Hold a valid license or registration in the state they are physically located in, if that state requires it
- Notify the Credentialing team within **5 business days** of relocating, even temporarily
- Confirm that their malpractice coverage extends to the new work location

### Interstate Medical Licensure Compact (IMLC) and Nurse Licensure Compact (NLC)

Daisy Health participates in compact licensure programs where available. Clinicians covered under the IMLC or NLC may practice in compact member states without obtaining a separate full license. Employees should confirm their compact eligibility with the Credentialing team before working from a new state.

### Telehealth Platform Requirements

All clinical encounters must be conducted through Daisy Health's approved telehealth platform. Clinical staff may not use personal video conferencing tools (e.g., Zoom personal accounts, FaceTime) for patient care, regardless of work location.

---

## 4. Data Security Requirements for Remote Work

All remote employees must comply with Daisy Health's Data Security Policy (HR-DS-002), which includes:

- Using a **Daisy Health-issued device** or an IT-approved personal device for all work
- Connecting through the **Daisy Health VPN** when accessing internal systems or patient records
- Never working from public Wi-Fi without VPN active
- Ensuring their home workspace is **private and secure** — no patient conversations in shared or public spaces
- Locking their device screen when stepping away

Clinical staff handling protected health information (PHI) must take additional precautions as outlined in the HIPAA and Data Security Policy (HR-DS-002).

---

## 5. Home Office Requirements

Employees are responsible for maintaining a safe, productive home workspace. Daisy Health provides a **one-time home office stipend of $500** for full-time employees to cover equipment such as a monitor, keyboard, desk, or chair. See the Expense Reimbursement Policy (HR-EX-003) for details on how to submit.

Employees must:

- Have a reliable high-speed internet connection (minimum 25 Mbps download)
- Have a dedicated, reasonably quiet workspace during scheduled work hours
- Ensure their workspace meets basic ergonomic safety standards

---

## 6. Temporary Relocation (Up to 4 Weeks)

Employees who wish to work from a different location temporarily — such as staying with family or traveling — may do so under the following conditions:

- The location is within an **approved state** (see Section 2)
- The employee notifies their manager at least **5 business days** in advance
- For clinical staff, licensure and credentialing requirements in Section 3 are met
- Duration does not exceed **4 consecutive weeks** without additional approval

Temporary relocation does not change the employee's official work location on record.

---

## 7. Extended Remote Relocation (More Than 4 Weeks)

Employees wishing to permanently or semi-permanently change their work location must submit a **Remote Work Location Change Request** through the HR portal. This process includes:

- Manager approval
- People Operations review (tax, benefits, payroll implications)
- IT security review
- For clinical staff: Credentialing team review

Processing takes up to **30 business days**. Employees should not relocate before receiving written approval. Unapproved relocation may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination.

---

## 8. Manager Responsibilities

Managers are responsible for:

- Ensuring their team members understand and comply with this policy
- Approving or denying temporary relocation requests within 3 business days
- Notifying People Operations of any team member location changes
- Monitoring for performance or security concerns related to remote work arrangements

---

## 9. Policy Violations

Violations of this policy, including working from an unapproved location or failing to notify People Operations of a location change, may result in:

- Formal written warning
- Suspension of remote work privileges
- Payroll or tax corrections at the employee's expense
- Termination for repeated or willful violations

---

## 10. Requesting an Exception

Employees who believe their situation warrants an exception to this policy should submit a written request to People Operations at people@daisyhealth.com. Exceptions are reviewed on a case-by-case basis and are not guaranteed.

